<TrustPage title="Editorial and sources policy | CalcsDesk" description="CalcsDesk’s approach to official sources, dated checks, AI assistance, calculator scope, review and corrections." breadcrumb="Editorial policy" eyebrow="The editorial standard" heading="The source comes before the claim." intro="CalcsDesk’s explanations and calculators should make their scope and evidence visible. A formula, date or professional-review statement must be supported by the work actually completed.">
  <h2>Use primary references.</h2><p>For taxes and payroll rules, start with the responsible authority’s publications and tools. Record the exact source and effective period with the rate data. Other calculator sites can inform usability research, but their results do not replace official evidence for the rates we use.</p><p>The <a href="/sources/">source directory</a> gives reference starting points. Only a calculator’s own sourced rate table explains which data that particular calculation uses.</p>
  <h2>Separate a source check from a review.</h2><p>A source check records that a value has been compared with the named publication. It does not mean a qualified professional has reviewed the calculator. The current federal tool identifies its automated checks and pending human review. Review roles, credentials and dates will be shown only for work that actually occurred.</p>
  <h2>Keep estimates inside their scope.</h2><p>Inputs, tax year, currency, assumptions and exclusions belong near the calculation. The first federal beta is a wage-only estimate of regular income tax before credits, with employee payroll taxes listed separately. It does not promise a paycheck, refund or complete tax-return result.</p><p>A result for an unsupported situation should not be presented as a personalised recommendation. General explanations and estimates are not tax, legal or financial advice.</p>
  <h2>Use AI assistance transparently.</h2><p>AI has assisted with implementation and draft explanations. It is not a source for changeable facts and does not confer a reviewer credential. Official-source comparisons, reproducible tests and human verification are separate steps. The current beta is not described as having passed the remaining human-review and full official-example release checks.</p>
  <h2>Test before full release.</h2><p>Calculator tests should cover official examples, thresholds, rounding, invalid inputs and meaningful edge cases. Published schedule cross-checks are useful, but they must not be relabelled as complete official worked examples when they are not. Selected limited betas are searchable and retain their scope and pending-review disclosures. Search eligibility is separate from full release or professional review. Tools still in preparation do not provide estimates.</p>
  <h2>Correct the record.</h2><p>When a material error is verified, the intended process is to fix the data or calculation, add a regression check where appropriate, and record the change with its date and affected scope. The <a href="/corrections/">corrections page</a> explains that process and the reporting channel’s current status.</p>
  <h2>Disclose funding where it exists.</h2><p>The current site carries no ad integration or affiliate links. If either is added later, it must be clearly labelled. No funding, sponsorship or expert endorsement is implied by the current beta.</p>
</TrustPage>
